Custom numeric sort is required for migration runners that use lexicographic ordering to avoid multi-digit prefix collisions (e.g., 100 before 9).
When implementing or debugging a database migration runner that orders files by name, especially if you plan to add multiple migrations with different digit counts in the same PR.
## The Problem
Many migration runners (including Go's `sort.Strings`) order files lexicographically. This causes "100_" to sort before "9_", breaking execution order when two new migrations are added in a single deploy.

## The Fix
Instead of renaming existing files to zero-padded names (which forces re-application on all environments), implement a custom comparator that extracts the numeric prefix from the filename and sorts by integer value.

```go
// In Go, replace sort.Strings(names) with:
sort.Slice(names, func(i, j int) bool { 
    return prefixInt(names[i]) < prefixInt(names[j]) 
})

func prefixInt(name string) int {
    // Extract number before first underscore
    s := strings.SplitN(name, "_", 2)[0]
    n, _ := strconv.Atoi(s)
    return n
}
```

## Why not rename?
Renaming files (e.g., `9.sql` to `09.sql`) changes the filename. Since most runners record applied migrations by filename in a schema table, renaming makes every migration appear "unapplied," causing them all to run again on staging/production.

## Verification
Always test with edge cases: 1-digit (9), 2-digit (10), and 3-digit (100) prefixes together.
